DIAGNOSTIC: dump the CSIPHY register space on stop

WHY
---
Same as with the CSID (patch 0078): everything the vendor writes has already
been verified one by one and it matches -lane table 115 of 115 against
csiphy_2ph_v1_2_reg, common block corrected to version 1.2, reset sequence,
interrupt masks with the exact pattern-, so what is missing is what is NOT
written and is not known to exist.

And there is one concrete measurement that can only be made with the whole dump:
COMPARING IT WITH THE SENSOR EMITTING AGAINST THE SENSOR SILENT.

The registers that change between those two captures are the ones that respond
to the real signal. That is where the only clue left lies about the question
that the whole investigation has gone without answering: whether the PHY ever
sees HIGH SPEED or only low-power transitions.

So far that question could only be attacked with the eleven status registers,
which are undocumented anywhere -not even Qualcomm decodes them, their driver
dumps them raw- and with the interrupt rate.

It goes behind a parameter because it is 288 reads and 72 lines of register.

This does NOT fix anything: it is to look.
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c	2026-08-17 14:38:20.963164673 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c	2026-08-17 14:38:20.979295927 +0200
@@ -1085,6 +1085,11 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(pulso_terminacion,
 	"Escribir 0x01 en CTRL33 (0x0884), el pulso de terminacion que mainline se deja. 1 = si.");
 
+static int volcado_csiphy;
+module_param(volcado_csiphy, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(volcado_csiphy,
+	"1 = volcar el espacio de registros del CSIPHY al parar la transmision.");
+
 static int releer_registros;
 module_param(releer_registros, int, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(releer_registros,
@@ -1487,6 +1492,35 @@
 				CSIPHY_3PH_CMN_CSI_COMMON_STATUSn(regs->offset,
 						regs->common_status_offset, i));
 
+	/*
+	 * VOLCADO COMPLETO DEL CSIPHY.
+	 *
+	 * Igual que con el CSID: ya se ha comprobado uno por uno todo lo que el
+	 * fabricante escribe y coincide, asi que lo que falta es lo que no se
+	 * escribe y no se sabe que existe.
+	 *
+	 * Lo interesante es COMPARAR el volcado con el sensor emitiendo contra
+	 * el sensor mudo: los registros que cambien son los que responden a la
+	 * senal de verdad, y ahi estaria la unica pista que queda sobre si el
+	 * PHY llega a ver alta velocidad o solo baja potencia.
+	 */
+	if (volcado_csiphy) {
+		int a;
+
+		for (a = 0; a < 0x900; a += 32)
+			dev_info(csiphy->camss->dev,
+				 "csiphy%d %03x: %08x %08x %08x %08x %08x %08x %08x %08x\n",
+				 csiphy->id, a,
+				 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x00),
+				 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x04),
+				 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x08),
+				 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x0c),
+				 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x10),
+				 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x14),
+				 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x18),
+				 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x1c));
+	}
+
 	dev_info(csiphy->camss->dev,
 		 "csiphy%d al parar: %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x\n",
 		 csiphy->id, diag_phy_estado[0], diag_phy_estado[1],
